UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 25, Lesson 4: Portfolio checkpoint', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A portfolio checkpoint means selecting strong work, explaining why it shows growth and setting a clear next goal.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nName one piece to keep and one next goal for handwriting or speaking.\n\nFEEDBACK\nKeep reason must link to a skill; goal should be specific and doable.\n\nCHECK\nKeeping everything without reflection is not a checkpoint.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nSelect three pieces (reading response, writing, speaking/handwriting). For each, write why it shows progress and one next goal. Share one reflection aloud.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"A portfolio checkpoint means selecting strong work, explaining why it shows growth and setting a clear next goal.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Speaking and handwriting","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-OLC-01","EN2-HANDW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Select three pieces (reading response, writing, speaking/handwriting). For each, write why it shows progress and one next goal. Share one reflection aloud. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: \"I chose my theme paragraph because my evidence improved. Next I will pause more when presenting speeches.\" Selection, reason, goal.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Keeping everything without reflection is not a checkpoint. Correction: Keep reason must link to a skill; goal should be specific and doable.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Keeping everything without reflection is not a checkpoint. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A portfolio checkpoint means selecting strong work, explaining why it shows growth and setting a clear next goal. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["A portfolio checkpoint means selecting strong work, explaining why it shows growth and setting a clear next goal.","Keeping everything without reflection is not a checkpoint.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Explain the evidence\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.","A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Keeping everything without reflection is not a checkpoint.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to an independent example\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Keep reason must link to a skill; goal should be specific and doable. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["Keep reason must link to a skill; goal should be specific and doable.","Keeping everything without reflection is not a checkpoint.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Name one piece to keep and one next goal for handwriting or speaking.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["The most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Underline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Keep reason must link to a skill; goal should be specific and doable. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["Keep reason must link to a skill; goal should be specific and doable.","A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Keeping everything without reflection is not a checkpoint.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a co\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["The new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Make one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant work with accurate reasoning and a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating the central skill, explaining the particular words or evidence that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: \"I chose my theme paragraph because my evidence improved. Next I will pause more when presenting speeches.\" Selection, reason, goal.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change meaning or accuracy, not just make the text longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus corrected example and purpose-linked explanation.","question":"Name one likely error in using this skill, correct it and explain why the correction helps.","sample_answer":"Common error: Keeping everything without reflection is not a checkpoint. Correction: Keep reason must link to a skill; goal should be specific and doable.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w25-l4","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise portfolio.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use portfolio with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify select and explain why it matters.","I can practise reflect independently on a new prompt.","I can notice goal needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A portfolio checkpoint means selecting strong work, explaining why it shows growth and setting a clear next goal. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A portfolio checkpoint means selecting strong work, explaining why it shows growth and setting a clear next goal.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"I chose my theme paragraph because my evidence improved. Next I will pause more when presenting speeches.\" Selection, reason, goal.\n\nTRY FIRST\nName one piece to keep and one next goal for handwriting or speaking.\n\nFEEDBACK\nKeep reason must link to a skill; goal should be specific and doable.","explain":"A portfolio checkpoint means selecting strong work, explaining why it shows growth and setting a clear next goal.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Keeping everything without reflection is not a checkpoint. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Portfolio piece chosen with a growth reason.","caption":"Selection needs reflection.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Select</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">theme paragraph kept</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Why</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">evidence improved</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: \"I chose my theme paragraph because my evidence improved. Next I will pause more when presenting speeches.\" Selection, r Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: \"I chose my theme paragraph because my evidence improved. Next I will pause more when presenting speeches.\" Selection, r Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"I chose my theme paragraph because my evidence improved. Next I will pause more when presenting speeches.\" Selection, reason, goal. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Specific goal contrasted with unreflective saving.","caption":"Name the next skill step.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Next goal</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">pause more in speeches</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Not checkpoint</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">keep everything, no goals</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nKeeping everything without reflection is not a checkpoint. Here is a more productive model: \"I chose my theme paragraph because my evidence improved. Next I will pause more when presenting speeches.\" Selection, reason, goal.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nKeep reason must link to a skill; goal should be specific and doable.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Keeping everything without reflection is not a checkpoint. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA portfolio checkpoint means selecting strong work, explaining why it shows growth and setting a clear next goal. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 25, Lesson 4: Portfolio checkpoint","unit_number":5,"unit_title":"Class novel study","week":25,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\n\"I chose my theme paragraph because my evidence improved. Next I will pause more when presenting speeches.\" Selection, reason, goal.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Portfolio piece chosen with a growth reason.","caption":"Selection needs reflection.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Select</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">theme paragraph kept</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Why</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">evidence improved</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Specific goal contrasted with unreflective saving.","caption":"Name the next skill step.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"1